I'm sure you've heard many variations on this theme:

Barbarian: 18/01 st, 17 co, 17 dx, dlev 6 clev 7, speed boots, ac -1, found
altar, telepathy, ring of invis, survived hallu, levi, destroy armor,
id'ed several other scrolls, 65 hps.

I.e.  A very promising character and game, especially with the speed
boots and ring of invisibility.

I had just "discovered" the scroll of punishment.  No problem.  I had
a sleeping nymph on the level.  I dropped everything I was carrying
except the heavy iron ball and went over to the nymph.  I kicked her
awake (kicking was unlikely to kill her).  She woke up and stole my
ball.  Great.  This is almost as nice as when I found the scroll of
remove curse immediately after the scroll of punishment.  No nasty
side effects, right?  Wrong.

I headed back, carrying absolutely nothing.  On the way back, in a
dark corridor, I bumped into a floating eye.  Due to type-ahead, I
took a swing at it with my knuckles.  Oops.  I missed.  It froze me
solid.  These two giant ants found this naked barbarian with a stupid
look on his face, smiled at each other, and said "hey, let's have some
fun!"  After chewing on him a few times, one noticed:  "It's not
moving!  I wonder why?  Still tastes good, anyhow."  They continued to
knaw until they ate him all up.  Scratch one barbarian.

Aaargh.

So what should be done about this?  I can think of one thing right
off:

There are several monsters (blobs, eyes, rust monsters) that can have
detrimental effects when you hit them.  Put in (perhaps as an option)
a feature with a little "are you sure?" before the first time you
attack one of these.  This would be very easy, since it's already done
for peaceful monsters.  It would save a lot of grief.  It's really
annoying to die because of one little slip.

Other places that could use an "are you sure" are moving into water,
stepping onto a trap that's already discovered.  Other possibles are
quaffing/reading items already discovered to be harmful.

The second thing would be to drop all my stuff right next to the nymph
and stand on it.  Unfortunately, this only works with sleeping
nymphs.

On the other paw, I realized something interesting.  The barbarian was
carrying a lot of good stuff.  However, he dropped it right before he
died.  What's more, the game left a bones level.  This means that all
of his good stuff is stll there, and it isn't cursed!!  So, you want 
your wizard to have good armor?  Save your game, and start a new one
with a knight.  Get the knight to a lower level, drop all your stuff,
and let someone kill you.  Once the wizard gets down there, he gets
the stuff (assuming he can deal with the ghost).  Tourists can supply
cameras.  Wizards can supply lots of good stuff.  Barbarians can drop
two-handed swords.  Thieves can drop lock picks.  Etc. etc.

It might even be a good idea to drop everything you're carrying if
you're in a situation where you know you're about to die anyway.  I
guess this doesn't work for armor.

I agree with the identify business.  They way it's done could use
improvement.

